# Which of Omarchy's default mise tools (etc/mise/conf.d/omarchy-tools.toml)
# omedora-settings declares system-wide on Fedora.
#
#   keep <key>  stays in /etc/mise/conf.d/omarchy-tools.toml (a lazy shim for every user)
#   drop <key>  is left out of it, so no user gets a shim for it on PATH
#
# Every AI coding agent is dropped. Omarchy's lazy shims put a `claude`, `kimi`,
# `codex`... ahead of ~/.local/bin and /usr/bin on every user's PATH. That
# shadows an install the user or their employer manages, and some workplaces
# don't allow these tools on a machine at all. The dropped declarations ship as
# reference material in
# /usr/share/omarchy/etc-overrides/mise/conf.d/omarchy-agent-tools.toml. An agent
# is still one menu action away (Setup > Defaults > Agent runs
# `mise use -g <tool>` for an undeclared tool).
#
# filter-mise-tools.sh fails the build on a key listed in neither column, so a
# rebase that adds a tool has to classify it here.

keep basecamp
keep gh
keep ghui
keep hey-cli
keep hunk
keep node
keep npm:cf
keep playwright
keep uv

drop amp
drop antigravity-cli
drop claude
drop codex
drop copilot
drop crush
drop cursor-agent
drop grok
drop http:devin
drop http:kiro-cli
drop muse
drop npm:@deepseek-ai/dsh
drop npm:@kilocode/cli
drop npm:@minimax-ai/code
drop npm:@moonshot-ai/kimi-code
drop npm:command-code
drop npm:freebuff
drop oh-my-pi
drop opencode
drop ori
drop pi
drop qwen
